--TEST--
Core\Storage's four rows agree about whether a key names an object, each in its own vocabulary — absence is null, a failed delete, an omission from the listing and a claim that succeeds — `rule:programs/framework-core-half`, ADR 0063 R7
--FILE--
<?nvs
// One question — "does this key name an object on this disk?" — asked of all
// four rows, and what is asserted is that they **agree**. Each answers in a
// different vocabulary, so a row that grew its own idea of absence still reads
// plausibly on its own line: `get` would answer `null` for a key `list` shows,
// or `delete` would quietly succeed over nothing. Counting the four together is
// what catches that; the numbers between 0 and 4 are the whole failure surface.
//
// The vocabularies, from `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/storage.rs`'s reference cards:
// `get` answers absence as `null` (ADR 0063 R7), `delete` fails over a key that
// was never there, `list` omits it, and `put` under `overwrite: false` is a
// claim, so it succeeds exactly where there is nothing to replace.
final class Ask {
    public static function byGet(string $key): bool {
        if (Core\Storage::get("uploads", $key) == null) {
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static function byList(string $key): bool {
        foreach (Core\Storage::list("uploads") as string $entry) {
            if ($entry == $key) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    // The two mutating rows put back what they took, so the four questions are
    // asked of one disk state rather than of four successive ones.
    public static function byPut(string $key): bool {
        try {
            Core\Storage::put("uploads", $key, "hello\n" as bytes, {overwrite: false});
        } catch (Throwable $error) {
            return true;
        }
        Core\Storage::delete("uploads", $key);
        return false;
    }

    public static function byDelete(string $key): bool {
        try {
            Core\Storage::delete("uploads", $key);
        } catch (Throwable $error) {
            return false;
        }
        Core\Storage::put("uploads", $key, "hello\n" as bytes);
        return true;
    }

    public static function agreeing(string $key): int {
        int $present = 0;
        if (Ask::byGet($key)) {
            $present = $present + 1;
        }
        if (Ask::byList($key)) {
            $present = $present + 1;
        }
        if (Ask::byPut($key)) {
            $present = $present + 1;
        }
        if (Ask::byDelete($key)) {
            $present = $present + 1;
        }
        return $present;
    }
}

Core\Storage::put("uploads", "note.txt", "hello\n" as bytes);

echo "nothing was put at ghost.txt: ", Ask::agreeing("ghost.txt"), " of 4 rows say it is there", "\n";
echo "an object is at note.txt: ", Ask::agreeing("note.txt"), " of 4 rows say it is there", "\n";

// And the disk is what it was, which is what makes the eight questions above
// eight readings of two states rather than a sequence of edits.
echo "still on the disk: ", Core\Str::join(Core\Storage::list("uploads"), ","), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
[storage.uploads]
root = "./objects"

[capabilities.fs]
read = ["./objects"]
write = ["./objects"]
--FILE objects/keep.txt--
the disk's root has to exist; this is what makes it
--EXPECT--
nothing was put at ghost.txt: 0 of 4 rows say it is there
an object is at note.txt: 4 of 4 rows say it is there
still on the disk: keep.txt,note.txt
